    "INDEX-1<SEP>Implement a binary search algorithm to find a specific element in a sorted array.<SEP>Here is a Python implementation of the binary search algorithm:\n\n```python\ndef binary_search(arr, low, high, x):\n \n    # Check base case\n    if high >= low:\n \n        mid = (high + low) // 2\n \n        # If element is present at the middle itself\n        if arr[mid] == x:\n            return mid\n \n        # If element is smaller than mid, then it can only be present in left subarray\n        elif arr[mid] > x:\n            return binary_search(arr, low, mid - 1, x)\n \n        # Else the element can only be present in right subarray\n        else:\n            return binary_search(arr, mid + 1, high, x)\n \n    else:\n        # Element is not present in array\n        return -1\n \n# Test array\narr = [2, 3, 4, 10, 40]\nx = 10\n \n# Function call\nresult = binary_search(arr, 0, len(arr)-1, x)\n \nif result != -1:\n    print(\"Element is present at index\", str(result))\nelse:\n    print(\"Element is not present in array\")\n```\n\nIn this Python code, the binary_search function takes four arguments - arr (the sorted array), low (the starting index of the array), high (the ending index of the array), and x (the element to be searched). The function first checks if the high index is greater than or equal to the low index, which is the base case. If the element is present at the middle of the array, it returns the middle index. If the element is smaller than the middle element, it recursively calls the binary_search function on the left subarray. If the element is larger than the middle element, it recursively calls the binary_search function on the right subarray. If the element is not present in the array, it returns -1.": 4.21875,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>Implement a binary search algorithm to find a specific element in a sorted array.<SEP>Sure, here is a Python implementation of a binary search algorithm:\n\n```python\ndef binary_search(arr, low, high, x):\n \n    # Check base case\n    if high >= low:\n \n        mid = (high + low) // 2\n \n        # If element is present at the middle itself\n        if arr[mid] == x:\n            return mid\n \n        # If element is smaller than mid, then it can only\n        # be present in left subarray\n        elif arr[mid] > x:\n            return binary_search(arr, low, mid - 1, x)\n \n        # Else the element can only be present in right subarray\n        else:\n            return binary_search(arr, mid + 1, high, x)\n \n    else:\n        # Element is not present in array\n        return -1\n```\n\nThe binary search algorithm works by repeatedly dividing the search interval in half. If the value of the search key is less than the item in the middle of the interval, the algorithm continues the search in the lower half. Otherwise, it continues in the upper half. This process continues until the value is found or the interval is empty.\n\nYou can use it like this:\n\n```python\narr = [2, 3, 4, 10, 40]\nx = 10\n \n# Function call\nresult = binary_search(arr, 0, len(arr)-1, x)\n \nif result != -1:\n    print(\"Element is present at index\", str(result))\nelse:\n    print(\"Element is not present in array\")\n```": 3.75,
