    "INDEX-1<SEP>Implement a binary search algorithm to find a specific element in a sorted array.<SEP>Sure, here is a Python implementation of the binary search algorithm:\n\n```python\ndef binary_search(arr, low, high, x):\n \n    # Check base case\n    if high >= low:\n \n        mid = (high + low) // 2\n \n        # If element is present at the middle itself\n        if arr[mid] == x:\n            return mid\n \n        # If element is smaller than mid, then it can only\n        # be present in left subarray\n        elif arr[mid] > x:\n            return binary_search(arr, low, mid - 1, x)\n \n        # Else the element can only be present in right subarray\n        else:\n            return binary_search(arr, mid + 1, high, x)\n \n    else:\n        # Element is not present in array\n        return -1\n \n# Test array\narr = [2, 3, 4, 10, 40]\nx = 10\n \n# Function call\nresult = binary_search(arr, 0, len(arr)-1, x)\n \nif result != -1:\n    print(\"Element is present at index\", str(result))\nelse:\n    print(\"Element is not present in array\")\n```\nThis script defines a function binary_search that takes a sorted array and a target value as input, and returns the index of the target value in the array if it is present. If the target value is not found, it returns -1. The function works by dividing the array in half and checking the middle element. If the target value is equal to the middle element, its index is returned. If the target value is less than the middle element, the function is called recursively on the left half of the array. If the target value is greater than the middle element, the function is called recursively on the right half of the array. This process continues until the target value is found or until the array is divided into two single-element arrays.": 3.3125,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>Implement a binary search algorithm to find a specific element in a sorted array.<SEP>In Python, a binary search algorithm can be implemented as follows:\n\n```python\ndef binary_search(arr, low, high, x):\n    if high >= low:\n        mid = (high + low) // 2\n        if arr[mid] == x:\n            return mid\n        elif arr[mid] > x:\n            return binary_search(arr, low, mid - 1, x)\n        else:\n            return binary_search(arr, mid + 1, high, x)\n    else:\n        return -1\n\n# Test the function\narr = [2, 3, 4, 10, 40]\nx = 10\n\nresult = binary_search(arr, 0, len(arr)-1, x)\n\nif result != -1:\n    print(\"Element is present at index\", str(result))\nelse:\n    print(\"Element is not present in array\")\n```\n\nIn this code, the function binary_search is defined to search for the element x in the sorted array arr. If the array is empty or contains only one element, the function will return -1 as the element is not found. Otherwise, the function will find the middle element of the array and compare it with the target element x. If the middle element is equal to x, the function will return the index of the middle element. If the middle element is greater than x, the function will recursively call itself with the left half of the array as the new input. If the middle element is smaller than x, the function will recursively call itself with the right half of the array as the new input.": 2.921875,
    "INDEX0<SEP>Implement a binary search algorithm to find a specific element in a sorted array.<SEP>Here is a Python implementation of the binary search algorithm with additional comments and descriptive variable names:\n\n```python\ndef binary_search(sorted_list, start_index, end_index, target_element):\n \n    # Check if inputs are valid\n    if not isinstance(sorted_list, list) or not all(isinstance(item, int) for item in sorted_list):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list must contain only integers.\")\n    if not isinstance(start_index, int) or not isinstance(end_index, int) or not isinstance(target_element, int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Start index, end index, and target element must be integers.\")\n    if start_index < 0 or end_index >= len(sorted_list):\n        raise ValueError(\"Start index and end index must be within the bounds of the list.\")\n    if start_index > end_index:\n        raise ValueError(\"Start index cannot be greater than end index.\")\n \n    # Check base case\n    if end_index >= start_index:\n \n        mid_index = (end_index + start_index) // 2\n \n        # If element is present at the middle itself\n        if sorted_list[mid_index] == target_element:\n            return mid_index\n \n        # If element is smaller than mid, then it can only be present in left subarray\n        elif sorted_list[mid_index] > target_element:\n            return binary_search(sorted_list, start_index, mid_index - 1, target_element)\n \n        # Else the element can only be present in right subarray\n        else:\n            return binary_search(sorted_list, mid_index + 1, end_index, target_element)\n \n    else:\n        # Element is not present in array\n        return -1\n```\n\nIn this Python code, the binary_search function takes four arguments - sorted_list (the sorted list), start_index (the starting index of the list), end_index (the ending index of the list), and target_element (the element to be searched). The function first checks if the inputs are valid, raising a ValueError if any of them are not. The function then checks the base case: if the end_index is greater than or equal to the start_index. If the element is present at the middle of the list, it returns the mid_index. If the element is smaller than the middle element, it recursively calls the binary_search function on the left subarray. If the element is larger than the middle element, it recursively calls the binary_search function on the right subarray. If the element is not present in the list, it returns -1.": 1.875,
